findings.body:
`formatStudiesText` calls `String(format: strings.detailTextStudies, appName)`, which is the v1 string ("%@ may install and run studies from time to time."). The PR adds `detailTextStudiesV2` ("%@ randomly selects users to test features…") for the split toggle, but the helper ignores it. Whoever wires this helper into the split-toggle PR will get the old copy, and nothing in this PR would catch it because there are no callers or tests.

The Rollouts formatter reads the matching new constant (`detailTextRollouts`), so the two helpers are inconsistent with each other.

::diff{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="417-430"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The Studies helper should read `detailTextStudiesV2` (or the helper should be removed, see the companion finding).

The `SettingsTextFormatter` singleton, `labelStudiesV2`, `detailTextStudiesV2`, `labelRollouts`, `detailTextRollouts`, and `detailTextSendUsageDataV2` are not referenced anywhere in the repo (I checked with a repo-wide grep). The existing Studies row in `SettingsViewController.swift:127-128` still uses `labelStudies`/`detailTextStudies`. The helper also duplicates `String(format:)` with no state and no tests. The PR description says this is a base for #30988, so the unused code is intentional, but it lands in the tree without any verification.

If the split PR is going to supply the call sites anyway, it's cleaner to add these constants there, where they can be exercised. Otherwise the reviewer has to judge the helper's correctness without seeing it in use, which is how the wrong-key bug above got through.

::code{file="focus-ios/Blockzilla/UIComponents/UIConstants.swift" lines="416-430"}

::callout{type="info"}
The checklist says "wrote tests for new code", and the helper has no tests.

The new exclusion `firefox-ios.xliff:Settings.Studies.Message.v148` silences the quotes check for that key. The English source ("%@ randomly selects users to test features, which improves quality for everyone.") contains no quote characters, so the exclusion isn't needed to pass the source string. The PR doesn't say why it was added, and an exclusion with no reason makes the lint silently weaker for that key. A translator who introduces straight or mismatched quotes in that message won't be flagged.

::diff{file="/.github/l10n/linter_config_ios.json" lines="8-10"}

If there's a real translation-side problem (e.g. a locale that needs straight quotes), the PR should say so in the description or a comment. Otherwise the exclusion should be dropped.
